{"_timestamps":{"start":"2026-05-29T12:46:25.422Z","slide_1":"2026-05-29T13:03:37.926Z","slide_2":"2026-05-29T13:15:26.856Z","slide_2_5":"2026-05-29T13:33:03.954Z","slide_3":"2026-06-01T04:49:58.593Z","slide_4":"2026-06-01T05:08:10.819Z","slide_5":"2026-06-01T05:17:43.396Z","slide_6":"2026-06-01T15:44:19.308Z"},"slide_1":"{"part_0":"Annie's parents were Joseph and Lena Googoo, and her home community was Sipeknekatik First Nation.","part_1":"The note written on the back says, \"Annie & Mary, summer before everything changed\" The photograph shows Annie and a girl named Mary standing together on a wooden porch. This shows that something significant happened after that summer.","part_2":"The assigned name that replaced Annie's name was Anne Peters.  The instructor noted that she was \"Exceptionally quite, refuses to speak, language barrier.\" This suggest she may have struggled because of language differences or the pressure to stop using her own culture."}","_telemetry":{"slide_1":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":836,"duration_sec":1033},"slide_2":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":658,"duration_sec":671},"slide_2_5":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":685,"duration_sec":736},"slide_3":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":611,"duration_sec":1872},"slide_4":{"pasted":true,"keystrokes":472,"duration_sec":177},"slide_5":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":434,"duration_sec":570},"slide_6":{"pasted":false,"keystrokes":1788,"duration_sec":1946}},"slide_2":"Annie spent time running along the Shubenacadie River, picking sweetgrass with her grandmother, and learning how to smudge, which shows her string connection to her culture and community. Her mother always said, "Kinship is a circle that cannot be cut," meaning family and community ties are important. The Mi'kmaq governed themselves through their own territory, district leaders, local chiefs, and the Grand Council, showing they have organized systems of leadership long before colonial governments arrived.","slide_2_5":"The shared ancestors at the top of the family tree are Joseph and Lena Googoo, Annie and Mary's parents from Sipeknekatik First Nation. Family unity was disrupted in different ways on both branches. On Mary's Branch, statues loss under the Indian Act affected legal identity and connections to community across generations. One Annie's branch, child welfare apprehension separated her from her family, language and culture after she was taken away and renamed Anne Peters. Even though polices are different but both caused separation and weakened family connection.","slide_3":"{"words":["overcrowding","lack","of","resource","management","immediate","removal"],"rationale":"\"Overcrowding\"focuses only on the living conditions without explaining that Indigenous families faced overcrowding because of government policies, housing and reserve conditions.                      \n\"Lack of resource management\"  This phrase blames the parents for poverty instead of recognizing that many families had limited access to jobs, resources, and support.\n\"Immediate removal\"This shows that a systems immediately separated children from their families instead of trying to help improve conditions at home."}","slide_4":"Three fragments Annie remembers from the day she was taken are a car, her mother crying, and a hand pulling her toward the road. The phrase “best interests” was used with assumptions that non-Indigenous nuclear families were better than Indigenous kinship networks, that poverty meant parents were failing, and that European-style parenting and lifestyles were the proper way to raise children. These beliefs ignored Indigenous cultures and family systems. The 1951 changes to the Indian Act gave provinces authority over Indigenous child welfare,which allowed provincial child welfare agencies to remove children from their communities. As a result, approximately 20,000 Indigenous children were removed during that became known as the Sixties Scoop.","slide_5":"{"ans_0":"This name change ignored Annie's identity, family, connections, language, community, and connection to Sipenknekatik. Changing her name symbolized the erasure of her culture and identity","ans_1":"A child in foster may act this way because they lost their family, home, language, culture, and sense of belonging. Her behavior could reflect trauma and separation rather that something being wrong with her."}","slide_6":"Annie Googoo was a young Mi'kmaw girl from Sipeknekatik First Nation who grew up surrounded by family, culture, and community before being taken from home during the Sixties Scoop. After being removed by child welfare authorities, she was renamed Anne Peters, separated from her family, and pushed to integrate into English culture. Colonial policies created the conditions later used to justify her removal because decades of reserve confinement, poverty, residential schools, and government control caused by overcrowding and limited resources in many Indigenous communities. Instead of addressing these problems, authorities blames families and used child welfare systems to remove children. 

Maya's family story connects to Sarah's because both show different forms of colonial control within the same family line. Sarah's branch experienced status loss through Indian Act, where laws decided who counted legally as Indigenous, while Annie;s branch experienced family separation through child welfare removal. Although the methods were different, both policies weakened family connections, and identity. 

Maya asks students rather than government records to help find the truth because official files often reduce people to categories, labels, and reports while ignoring their humanity and experiences. History told through families, survivors, and communities gives a fuller picture. The survivors stories and problems should not be told by government, because they might not define it correctly, because they don't really understand what truly went threw."}

{"ans-q1-risk":"Indigenous adults make up about 32–33% of federal custody admissions even though they are only about 5% of Canada’s population. In the 2017 class notes, Indigenous men were 25.2% of male inmates and Indigenous women were 36.1% of female inmates, showing that the problem has continued to grow. The actuarial scoring system is unfair because it labels people as “high risk” based on poverty, unemployment, or housing problems instead of actual criminal intent. This causes many Indigenous and marginalized people to stay in custody longer before trial.","ans-q2-trc":"In Sugar Falls, the students visit Kokum (Grandma Betty) to learn about her experience in residential school. Before she tells her story, she smudges to help clear her mind and feel strong because the memories are painful. A government or church worker later comes by canoe to take Betty away to residential school. Her mother also went to residential school, so she has trauma and struggles to show love, which affects her relationship with Betty. The drum beat feels strong and emotional, and it represents Indigenous culture, ancestors, and survival. At the end, Betty’s father says she has to go because “it’s the law,” showing they had no choice.","ans-q3-marshall":"Donald Marshall Jr. was a Mi’kmaw youth who was wrongly convicted of murder in Sydney, Nova Scotia. The Royal Commission said the justice system failed him because of unfair treatment, mistakes, and racism against Indigenous people.","ans-q4-gladue":"Substantive equality means courts must look at an Indigenous person’s background and life experiences to make fair decisions instead of treating everyone exactly the same. The “Gladue funding gap” is when there is not enough money for Indigenous housing, healing, or mental health programs, so judges may not have good alternatives to jail and end up giving prison sentences instead.","ans-q5-circle":"A Sentencing Circle is different from a regular courtroom because everyone sits in a circle at the same level with no judge above others and no robes, showing equality and respect. In Mi’kmaw culture, the Eagle Feather is important because the person holding it has the chance to speak and be listened to with honesty and respect.","ans-q6-comparison":"Conventional courts focus on deciding guilt and giving punishment. The victim usually has a small role, and the court makes the decisions.

Sentencing circles focus on healing the harm caused by the crime. The victim, offender, Elders, and community all take part in finding a solution.

The victim has a bigger role because they can speak directly about how they were affected. As the Fact Check says, restorative justice asks the hardest thing of the victim: being willing to sit in the same room as the person who harmed them. The community also helps support healing and accountability.","ans-q7-recidivism":"","ans-q8-relations":"","ans-q9-maya":"","ans-q10-leo":"","_timestamps":{"start":"2026-05-29T13:31:00.085Z"},"_telemetry":{"session":{"keystrokes":2,"pasted":true,"duration_sec":168}}}

